{
	title: '/config: settings as a form',
	spec: 'Bare /config opens the current settings table (src/common/settings.ts) as one durable question form: each setting a field of its type (text, secret masked, integer, choice; booleans choose true/false), non-secret values prefilled from config, its one-line description as help. Integer fields edit a string and use a numeric keyboard on web; the settings table validates their range. Secret fields start empty, never expose the existing value, and leaving them empty keeps it. Submitting writes only fields changed from the form baseline to config.ason through its liveFile, keeping unknown keys and comments; unchanged fields do not overwrite edits made since the form opened. Changed keys set to their default are removed rather than stored. /config <name> <value> sets one directly, /config <name> shows it; an empty quoted value clears a text setting that permits empty text. Validation is the table’s: a bad value is refused with the reason before recording an answer, and the original form stays open with its edited values. Secrets are never echoed back in output, warnings, or history (answers record only that a secret was given; direct command text redacts the value). A malformed config is never overwritten. Same question and behavior on terminal and web.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Uses the existing durable command question, like /login, as this task requests (rather than the future client-only /config modal mentioned in w4/forms.md). Secret prefilling is deliberately omitted: form definitions are history records, so prefilling would leak the credential.',
		'commands.checkAnswers runs before prompts.reply records an answer. commands.record sanitizes direct secret arguments before command history or broadcast. The non-secret baseline travels in askArgs so restart preserves changed-only semantics.'
	],
	needs: ['pc', 'w4']
}
